            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "@justinmk \r\n\r\nI dug into trying to do this at a lower level in C, but it's actually a no-go with LuaJIT unless we want to entirely replace `pairs`.\r\n\r\nIn standard Lua 5.3+, `luaL_typeerror` uses `luaL_typename`, which checks the metatable for a `__name` field when it builds the error message. But in LuaJIT (and Lua 5.1), `luaL_typerror` and `luaL_checktype` just hardcode the raw type lookup. They unconditionally spit out `userdata` for all userdata objects and don't care at all about the metatable's `__name` or `__tostring`.\r\n\r\nSince the error gets thrown from deep inside LuaJIT's native C/assembly implementation of `pairs`, there's no hook for `vim.NIL` to jump in and change what it reports natively.\r\n\r\nThe only \"low-level\" way to pull this off would be to completely override the global `pairs`, `ipairs`, and `next` with our own custom C functions. Doing it in Lua (like this PR does) is basically doing the exact same thing but with way less boilerplate.\r\n\r\nShould we just stick with the Lua wrappers here, or would you rather we drop the error-message enhancement entirely and just let it throw the standard `table expected, got userdata` error?",
            "created_at": "2026-07-16T14:17:17Z",
            "html_url": "https://github.com/neovim/neovim/pull/40764#issuecomment-4992946817",
            "id": 4992946817,
            "issue_url": "https://api.github.com/repos/neovim/neovim/issues/40764",
            "minimized": null,
            "node_id": "IC_kwDOAPphoM8AAAABKZpSgQ",
            "performed_via_github_app": null,
            "reactions": {
                "+1": 0,
                "-1": 0,
                "confused": 0,
                "eyes": 0,
